---
/* =========================================================================================
   THE STATED SITUATION, BEFORE ANY CHART AND BEFORE ANY TABLE.

   WHAT WALKING THE SITE AS A READER FOUND. Every data page opened with method: how the venues
   settle, what the tint means, what is and is not collected, why the number is trustworthy. All
   of it true, none of it an answer. On a 375px screen /funding spent its entire first screen on
   a ten-line paragraph about interval ratios, three totals nobody arrives asking for, and a
   six-line key to two colours that were not yet on screen — and a reader who came to find out
   what funding is doing right now reached the bottom of that screen without seeing a single
   funding rate. /liquidations was worse: twenty lines of prose and two dropdowns, with the map
   the page exists for entirely below the fold.

   A CHART IS NOT AN ANSWER EITHER. It shows a shape over time and reading it costs
   interpretation and some chart literacy. What a person needs in the first two seconds is a
   sentence: what is true right now, and whether it is unusual. The picture underneath is
   evidence for that sentence rather than a substitute for it.

   SO THIS IS PROSE, NOT A METRIC ROW. The metric row is what it replaces — "Contracts 50 /
   Open interest $9.48B / 24h volume $9.66B" took the most valuable band on four pages and
   answered nothing anyone came for. A number that is not part of the conclusion does not belong
   above the conclusion.

   TWO RULES FOR WHAT GOES IN IT, both learned from the same walk:

   DIRECTION IN WORDS, NOT ONLY IN COLOUR. "Longs are paying" is instant and needs no key.
   A positive number is a convention a reader has to have been taught, and the page that teaches
   it does so in a legend they have not reached yet.

   A MAGNITUDE NEEDS SOMETHING TO BE JUDGED AGAINST. A rate alone cannot be read as high or low.
   Where it sits against its own recent range is what makes it meaningful, and this site holds
   200 daily readings per contract — the funding column of the candle series every symbol page
   already renders — so the comparison is arithmetic on data we have, not a stub.
   ========================================================================================= */
interface Props {
  /** Set on pages whose situation is derived from a model rather than observed. */
  modelled?: boolean;
  class?: string;
}
const { modelled = false, class: cls } = Astro.props;
---

<p class:list={["overview", modelled && "overview--modelled", cls]}><slot /></p>

<style is:global>
  /* LARGER THAN THE BODY AND IN THE TEXT COLOUR, because it is the thing to read first and the
     explanation below it is not. The page's own <h1> stays the heading; this is the sentence
     under it, which is the shape a broadsheet standfirst has always had.

     NOT `.lede`, WHICH IS TAKEN. The homepage already uses .lede for its 21px h1 treatment, and
     this file's own history contains a class name that meant two things on two pages — see the
     .callout note in Base.astro. One name, one treatment. */
  .overview {
    margin: 0 0 var(--gap-region);
    font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--text);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }
  /* The numbers inside a stated situation are the situation. Dimming the prose around them is
     what makes it read as a sentence rather than as a wall of bold — the same treatment the
     homepage lede uses, applied the other way round because here the prose is the majority. */
  .overview b, .overview strong { font-weight: 600; color: var(--text); }
  .overview a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
  .overview a:hover { text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px; }
  /* A MODELLED SITUATION SAYS SO IN THE MARGIN, not only in a disclaimer further down. The
     liquidation pages state a conclusion drawn from assumptions, and a reader who takes the
     first sentence away with them should take that with it. */
  .overview--modelled { border-left: 2px solid var(--border); padding-left: var(--gap-block); }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    /* 16px is the floor at which iOS does not zoom on focus and at which a standfirst still
       reads as one. Below that this becomes body text with extra weight, which is not the job. */
    .overview { font-size: 16px; }
  }
</style>
